hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in book 4 of  Plato's Republic he is going to near the  end discuss the nature of injustice  within the human soul or personality  within the human person what's going on  when we not only do the wrong things but  we we choose to do the wrong things it  becomes part of who we are we become  licentiousness of actions like arguing  with our neighbors more often than we  should over stupid things what's  actually going on there and Plato is not  going to provide us with the complete  discussion of this that's deferred until  later on in the Republic in book 8  within which there's specific  discussions of this type and this type  and this type but in general what he's  going to say is that injustice is not  just the result of but is actually  constituted by a disharmony within the  soul and this is analogous to a kind of  disharmony within a political community  in which different occupational classes  don't work well with each other and get  into conflict and some  even faction and civil war with each  other now in this this section he brings  up a number of different examples of  injustice prior to to going into what  the shape of injustice is he talks about  a good person and the things that they  won't do they're not going to withhold  and embezzle a deposit of gold or silver  right so they're not engaging in a kind  of theft or betrayal of their agreements  there they're not going to be faithless  in keeping of oaths or other agreements  they're not going to engage in adultery  neglect of parents neglect of the due  service of the gods and you know we can  think of all sorts of other things as  well the point is every bit of injustice  every kind of doing wrong for Plato can  be traced back to this and it connects  with his notion of the tripartite soul  so let's remind ourselves what that  means Plato thinks that just like within  the ideal city there is the function of  the guardians there is analogous to that  the highest part of the soul which is  the intellectual or rational part though  you know the the logic a part right the  one that has logos the one that can  think things out and make good decisions  and that part is the the part that  should be ruling then there's the  spirited part of the soul spirited there  of course translating Lumos which is a  difficult Greek term to adequately  render into English it's sometimes  translated as the passionate part of the  soul but you don't want to strictly  identify it with what we call passion  today and that's a middle part of the  soul the part that gets angry or riled  up the part that helps to defend us  against outside threats and in Justices  and the part that can also work within  us either for or against the other parts  of the soul it's the part that gives us  the energy to get things done you might  say then we have and here in this  discussion Plato is going to talk about  in the singular but it's really in the  plural the  appetites the epitome me I in Greek  these are desires for things like eating  and drinking and having sex and laying  around and looking at Netflix all day  plain of course doesn't talk about  Netflix but that would fit in there as  well I think because it would be an  appetite and the appetites cannot  control themselves they need the  rational and the spirited part to  provide them with some sort of guidance  some sort of limits and to enforce those  limits upon them so what Plato sets out  for us is the ideal of a well a well  oriented a well ordered a well  structured soul which is really that of  a harmony and he actually does use a  musical metaphor here he talks about  having first attained a self mastery and  beautiful order within himself and  having harmonized these three parts or  principles the notes or intervals of  three terms quite literally the lowest  the highest and the mean and all others  there may be between them and having  linked and bound all three together and  made of himself a unity one person  instead of many self-controlled and in  unison this is the way that that Plato  depicts justice within the soul so how  should we then understand acting  unjustly or engaging in injustice  producing injustice and even worse being  unjust so here there's a lot of  interesting metaphors and again these  these can be analogized to the big city  and then brought back to the individual  soul Plato talks about there being a  civil war  of the three parts against each other  and in in terms of the city this is  fairly easy to understand again you  might want to look at book 8 where  there's more specific discussion about  how this takes place how the different  groups within the society find  themselves at odds with each other and  conflict with each other in terms of the  individual soul what happens is  the rationale the spirited and the  appetite of parts of the soul do not get  on the same page and are not able to  harm mo harmoniously work together each  of them doing their their proper  function and so we have this break down  into a civil war or a faction you might  say that that it ties in with each of  them attempting to do what is not its  function or to make the other parts  serve them in ways that they they  shouldn't now notice that Plato is not  saying well each part is like its own  little thing by itself compartmentalized  off and none of them should actually  tell the other what to do as a matter of  fact when the souls working rightly as  we said the rational part tells  everybody what to do the spirited part  enforces it and the appetites do what  they do within the limits set out by  that so it's not as if it's anarchy  right or compartmentalization instead  it's supposed to be this this this way  of ruling but there's all sorts of ways  in which this can go wrong so the  spirited part of the soul could be  driving the person and subordinating the  rational part to its desires for honor  social status the appetites could make  it the the soul a sort of you know  conform to the human notion that reason  is and can only ever be the slave of the  passions as a matter of fact there's  room for the human viewpoint it's just  that the human viewpoint from a platonic  standpoint is going to be engaged in  injustice within the the soul right and  even the rational part could could go  wrong in certain ways although Plato  doesn't talk that much about that he  uses other language that I think is  worth dwelling on and considering  because it helps us figure out what's  going on here  they meddle with each other they look  over each other's work and say you  should be doing things this way right  and they're not supposed to be doing  that other than the rational part  looking looking over them  they interfere with each other they get  in the way of each other they hinder  each other so that they don't do their  proper function here's where we could  think of an example what if the rational  part actually had wrongheaded ideas very  aesthetic ideas and said appetites you  shouldn't indulge yourself at all not  only is sex off the table and toxicants  are off the table and the food that you  eat is going to be the bare minimum to  survive when there's actually no need to  do that well that would be interference  wouldn't it revolt they revolt and they  attempt to you know place themselves  over the others reason you know could be  imperious in some respect the spirited  part could try to take control the  appetites can try to take control  confusion confusing one for the other  mixing themselves up this is what  happens within a human being and this  produces vices the vices of  foolishness or ignorance you know which  is opposed to that of wisdom the vice of  cowardice which is opposed to the vice  of courage the vice of intemperance or  self-indulgence which is opposed to the  vice of temperance or moderation and  then finally the vice itself of  injustice which leads us to doing all  these bad behaviors like committing  adultery or breaking our our agreements  with people or robbing temples as  another famous thing it's sort of like  robbing banks in ancient times because  temples are often a place where deposits  were made so the vices the vices then  can be understood using this model as a  sort of disease a disease you know by  analogy to virtue being health health  being a proper ordering of the body  disease of the soul ugliness  shamefulness is another way of  translating it a lack of nobility a lack  of what it makes things attractive  that's what what vices are  weaknesses this is another great way to  think about vices a vice is a bad habit  but it's also a susceptibility to make  the wrong decisions based on the  attractiveness or the repugnance of  things that are largely outside of  ourselves the person whose appetites are  in charge and too strong well they can  be led around by sex or by food or buy  you know intoxication or whatever else  it is  they can also be led around by a desire  for security or a desire to not have to  do work the person who is being led  around by their spirited parts it could  be cowardice right but it could also be  a tendency to be too aggressive with  people you know to compete with in the  social arena and bring to a more honor  to oneself than is actually deserved  insisting on reinforcing one's own  social status at the expense of others  that's a weakness so we can go on with  this what what do these do what is the  problem with us well they make a person  sole dis harmonious our souls are  already dis harmonious and the goal of  you might say platonic virtue ethics is  to make us move more and more towards  being an actual unity but we can also go  in the other direction where we feel as  if we're at war with with ourselves we  we can't be the same person from moment  to moment and the result of this is that  the life that one's living even if you  picture it to yourself as one that  encompasses many things that are  understood as goods some of which may be  genuine good some of which may be  apparent Goods one's life is not good as  Plato says through Socrates one's life  is not worth living the life of the  person who is addicted to fame is not  really worth living even if they do  manage to become famous for a while the  life of the greedy person the life of  the person who is always you know giving  in to fear is  not really a fully human life and  therefore is not a good life so what  we've got here is a depiction of how  injustice works within the soul and how  it's connected to these different parts  of the soul and Plato's tripartite  psychology engaging in overreaching and  engaging in faction or civil war  you
 

